'' The implicit main.
''
'' The shape was MEASURED from fbc's own C intermediate for the main translation unit
'' ('fbc -r -m'), not assumed -- see docs/internals/phase-6-main.md:
''
''     int32 main( int32 argc, char** argv ) {
''         int32 fb$result$0;  memset( &fb$result$0, 0, 4 );
''         fb_Init( argc, argv, 0 );
''         ... body ...  fb_StrDelete( &S$0 );  fb_End( 0 );
''         return fb$result$0;
''     }
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/main.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 scope imbalance

'' Two parameters, argc and argv, and an i32 return -- the process exit code.
'' EXPECT-OUT: func i32 main($0, $1)
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_Init($0, $1

'' Module-level DIM is a LOCAL of main, not a global: a module-level DIM without SHARED
'' is visible only at module level.
'' EXPECT-OUT: local i32 g

'' A string WITH an initialiser goes through fb_StrInit, not fb_StrAssign. Assign would
'' release what the destination already held, and at a declaration that is garbage.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_StrInit(@s, -1
'' EXPECT-NOT: fb_StrAssign(@s

'' A string WITHOUT one has its 24-byte descriptor zeroed. This is not cosmetic: the
'' fb_StrDelete below is registered either way, so an unwritten descriptor would hand
'' the runtime a wild pointer to free.
'' EXPECT-OUT: field.addr @t, 16
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_StrDelete(@t)

'' Cleanups run BEFORE fb_End, which is the order fbc emits; shutting the runtime down
'' first would invert it.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_End
'' EXPECT-NOT: <NO TERMINATOR>
'' GOLDEN: expected/main.out
